---
title: Moves
sidebar:
  order: 3
---

A **move** is something the player or a partner does on their turn, such as Power Jump or Headbonk. Each move has an entry in `registry/moves.yaml`, which sets its name, its cost, and what it can target, and an EVT script that plays it out. Each of the player's moves is its own file in `battle/move/`. A partner's moves are in its battle file instead; see [Partners](/papermario-dx/partners/).

The examples on this page make a jump move called Heavy Stomp, which the player gets from a badge. Use your own move's name and values in their place.

## Creating the move

Moves are grouped by category, with a folder for each:

| Folder | Holds | EVT script table |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `battle/move/jump` | Jump moves, such as Power Jump | `gMoveScriptTable` in `battle/use_moves.c` |
| `battle/move/hammer` | Hammer moves, such as Power Smash | `gMoveScriptTable` in `battle/use_moves.c` |
| `battle/move/star_power` | Star powers, such as Refresh | `StarPowersTable` in `battle/use_star_powers.c` |
| `battle/move/item` | What items do in battle, such as the Mushroom | `gBattleItemTable` in `battle/use_items.c` |

{% steps %}
1. In the Filesystem, right-click a category's folder in `battle/move`, such as `jump`, and select **New > Move**.
2. Enter a name for the move, such as `heavy_stomp`.
3. Select **OK**.
{% /steps %}

The new file has an empty `EVS_UseMove`, which runs when the player uses the move. The comment at the top of the file says which EVT script table the category uses. An item's file registers in `registry/items.yaml` and `gBattleItemTable` instead, as [Write a battle EVT script for the item](/papermario-dx/items/add-an-item/#writing-a-battle-evt-script-for-the-item) describes, so the rest of this page is about jump and hammer moves.

## Registering the move

{% steps %}
1. Add an entry to the end of `registry/moves.yaml`, named after your move in PascalCase:

   ```yaml
   - HeavyStomp:
       nameMsg: MSG_PlayerMoveName_HeavyStomp
       fullDescMsg: MSG_ItemFullDesc_HeavyStomp
       shortDescMsg: MSG_ItemShortDesc_HeavyStomp
       flags: [ TARGET_FLAG_SELECT_ONE, TARGET_FLAG_JUMP_LIKE, TARGET_FLAG_NOT_BELOW, TARGET_FLAG_ALLOW_TARGET_ONLY, TARGET_FLAG_NO_CEILING ]
       category: MOVE_TYPE_JUMP
       actionTip: BTL_MSG_ACTION_TIP_PRESS_BEFORE_LANDING
       costFP: 3
       costBP: 2
   ```

   An entry's position in the file is its ID, so always add new entries at the end. The entry's name decides its constant: `HeavyStomp` becomes `MOVE_HEAVY_STOMP`.

2. Add the messages that `nameMsg`, `fullDescMsg`, and `shortDescMsg` name. The game's own move names are in `msg/2A_PlayerMoves.msg`, full descriptions in `msg/25_FullItemDesc.msg`, and short descriptions in `msg/23_ShortItemDesc.msg`. See [Write messages](/papermario-dx/messages/edit-messages/).

3. In `battle/use_moves.c`, add a line for the move to the end of `gMoveScriptTable`:

   ```c
   [MOVE_HEAVY_STOMP]          MOVE_SCRIPT("heavy_stomp", 0),
   ```

   `MOVE_SCRIPT` takes the file's name and which EVT script in the file's `OVL_DEF_BATTLE_SCRIPT` to run, counting from `0`.
{% /steps %}

These are the fields of a move's entry:

| Field | What it sets |
| --- | --- |
| `nameMsg` | The move's name in menus. |
| `fullDescMsg` | For a partner's move, the description in the pause menu. A badge shows its own description from `registry/items.yaml` instead. |
| `shortDescMsg` | The description in the battle menu. |
| `flags` | Which actors the move can target, as `TARGET_FLAG_` flags. See [Choose targets](#choosing-targets). |
| `category` | Which battle menu offers the move: `MOVE_TYPE_JUMP`, `MOVE_TYPE_HAMMER`, `MOVE_TYPE_STAR_POWER`, or `MOVE_TYPE_PARTNER`. |
| `actionTip` | The action command instructions shown while the player chooses a target and as the move starts, such as `BTL_MSG_ACTION_TIP_PRESS_BEFORE_LANDING`, or `NONE`. |
| `costFP` | The FP the move costs. For a star power, the number of star power bars. |
| `costBP` | The BP it costs to equip the move's badge. |

## Writing the move's EVT script

This `EVS_UseMove` jumps on the target and deals 5 damage if the player succeeds at the action command, or 3 if they don't:

```c
#include "common.h"
#include "battle/script_module.h"
#include "script_api/battle.h"

#include "battle/common/move/JumpSupport.inc.c"

EvtScript EVS_UseMove = {
    Call(EnablePlayerBlur, ACTOR_BLUR_ENABLE)
    Call(ShowActionHud, true)
    // run up to the target and jump at it
    ExecWait(EVS_JumpSupport_ApproachAndJump)
    Call(PlayerTestEnemy, LVar0, DAMAGE_TYPE_JUMP, 0, 0, 1, 0)
    IfEq(LVar0, HIT_RESULT_MISS)
        ExecWait(EVS_JumpSupport_Miss)
        Call(EnablePlayerBlur, ACTOR_BLUR_DISABLE)
        Return
    EndIf
    Wait(1)
    Call(GetPlayerActionQuality, LVar0)
    Switch(LVar0)
        CaseGt(0)
            Call(PlayerDamageEnemy, LVar0, DAMAGE_TYPE_JUMP, 0, 0, 5,
                BS_FLAGS1_INCLUDE_POWER_UPS | BS_FLAGS1_TRIGGER_EVENTS | BS_FLAGS1_NICE_HIT)
        CaseDefault
            Call(PlayerDamageEnemy, LVar0, DAMAGE_TYPE_JUMP, 0, 0, 3,
                BS_FLAGS1_INCLUDE_POWER_UPS | BS_FLAGS1_TRIGGER_EVENTS)
    EndSwitch
    // bounce off the target and return home
    Switch(LVar0)
        CaseGt(HIT_RESULT_HIT)
            ExecWait(EVS_JumpSupport_Rebound)
        CaseDefault
            ExecWait(EVS_JumpSupport_WeakRebound)
    EndSwitch
    Call(EnablePlayerBlur, ACTOR_BLUR_DISABLE)
    Return
    End
};

OVL_DEF_BATTLE_SCRIPT(BATTLE_SCRIPT_KIND_MOVE, &EVS_UseMove);
```

`battle/common/move/JumpSupport.inc.c` has the EVT scripts that the game's jump moves share, such as running up to the target, jumping, and returning home. For a hammer move, `battle/common/move/HammerSupport.inc.c` has the same kind of EVT scripts. The game's own moves in `battle/move/` are good starting points to copy.

### Choosing targets

The move's `flags` in `registry/moves.yaml` decide which actors it can target. With `TARGET_FLAG_SELECT_ONE`, the player chooses one of them before the move starts, and `SetGoalToTarget` sets the player's goal position to it.

Without `TARGET_FLAG_SELECT_ONE`, the move targets every actor its flags allow. To hit each of them, step through the target list:

```c
Set(LVar9, 0)
Call(InitTargetIterator)
Label(0)
    Call(SetGoalToTarget, ACTOR_PLAYER)
    Call(PlayerDamageEnemy, LVar0, DAMAGE_TYPE_NO_CONTACT, 0, 0, 2, BS_FLAGS1_TRIGGER_EVENTS)
    Call(ChooseNextTarget, ITER_NEXT, LVar1)
    Add(LVar9, 1)
    Call(GetTargetListLength, LVar1)
    IfLt(LVar9, LVar1)
        Goto(0)
    EndIf
```

### Dealing damage

`PlayerTestEnemy` checks whether the move hits the target without hurting it, and `PlayerDamageEnemy` deals the damage. Both take the same arguments:

| Argument | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | The variable that receives the result, such as `HIT_RESULT_HIT` or `HIT_RESULT_MISS`. |
| 2 | The [damage type flags](/papermario-dx/battles/actors/attacks/#damage-type-flags), such as `DAMAGE_TYPE_JUMP`. |
| 3 | `SUPPRESS_EVENT_` flags for the target's reactions to skip, or `0`. |
| 4 | A status to inflict, such as `DMG_STATUS_KEY(STATUS_FLAG_SLEEP, 3, 75)` for a 75% chance of 3 turns of sleep, or `0`. |
| 5 | The damage. This is the only argument that can be a variable. |
| 6 | `BS_FLAGS1_` flags. |

These are the flags for the sixth argument:

| Flag | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `BS_FLAGS1_INCLUDE_POWER_UPS` | Adds Merlee's attack boost, and Power Rush or Mega Rush, to the damage. Jump damage gets them without this flag. |
| `BS_FLAGS1_TRIGGER_EVENTS` | Lets the hit set off the target's special reactions, such as being scared away by a fear attack. A move that hits more than once usually leaves it off every hit but the last. |
| `BS_FLAGS1_NICE_HIT` | Marks the hit as a successful action command, which shows **Nice** and counts for badges such as All or Nothing. |
| `BS_FLAGS1_NO_RATING` | Hides **Nice** and **Super**. |

A partner's moves use `PartnerTestEnemy` and `PartnerDamageEnemy`, which take the same arguments.

### Reading the action command's result

`EVS_JumpSupport_ApproachAndJump` loads and starts the jump action command. After the jump, `GetPlayerActionQuality` gives a number above `0` if the player succeeded, and `0` if they didn't. To give a move a different action command, see [Action commands](/papermario-dx/battles/action-commands/).

## Giving the move to the player with a badge

Jump and hammer moves come from badges. When the player equips a badge, its move appears in the battle menu that matches the move's `category`, and the badge's BP cost is the move's `costBP`.

To make a badge for the move, follow [Add the badge](/papermario-dx/items/add-a-badge/#adding-the-badge), but skip its `registry/moves.yaml` step, and set the badge's `moveID` to your move, such as `MOVE_HEAVY_STOMP`. The badge doesn't need an ability.

A badge's `moveID` is stored in a byte, so a move that a badge gives must be one of the first 256 entries in `registry/moves.yaml`.

## Replacing the basic jump or hammer

The first move in the jump and hammer menus depends on the player's boots and hammer: `MOVE_JUMP1`, `MOVE_JUMP2`, or `MOVE_JUMP3` for normal, Super, or Ultra boots, and `MOVE_HAMMER1` to `MOVE_HAMMER3` for the hammer. To change what one does, point its line in `gMoveScriptTable` at your file.

## Testing the move

{% steps %}
1. Start the game, and press **D-Pad Left** to open the [debug menu](/papermario-dx/debug/debug-menu/).
2. Select **Edit Inventory > Badges**, and set an empty slot to your badge's ID. The badge's name appears next to the ID once it's right.
3. Close the debug menu, then equip the badge from the **Badges** tab of the pause menu.
4. Open the debug menu again, and start a battle with **Battle Select**.
5. In the battle, choose the move from the jump menu.
{% /steps %}

If the player hasn't got the Lucky Star, action commands always fail. To give it to them, select **Edit Inventory > Equipment** in the debug menu and turn on **Lucky Star**.
